<description>
Remove blank separator columns and slide each vertical object group so adjacent connector endpoints touch.

Input structure
A short strip contains monochrome path-like object groups, each occupying a contiguous run of non-background columns; runs are separated by all-background columns. Each group has a unique degree-1 foreground endpoint on both its left and right outer edge.

Rule
Delete the all-background separator columns. Keep the first group in its original vertical position, then process the remaining groups from left to right: shift each group vertically, without rotation or recoloring, so its left endpoint is on the same row as the previous placed group's right endpoint, and paste it immediately to the right.

Key insight
The intended contacts are the degree-1 edge endpoints, not every possible foreground edge overlap, which makes the packed strip a single connected chain.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "monochrome path-like column groups s separated by all-background columns, ordered left to right",
  "dst": "adjacent packed column slots d in the same left-to-right order",
  "match_cond": "rank(s) == rank(d)",
  "transform": "remove separator columns; keep the first group at its original vertical offset, then paste each later group shifted vertically so its left degree-1 edge endpoint touches the previous placed group's right degree-1 edge endpoint",
  "multi_step": "after each placement, that group's right endpoint row becomes the connector row for the next group",
  "grid_shape": "same height as input; width is the sum of the non-background column-group widths"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 3 rows; width = sum(group widths) + (N-1) separator columns
  background: 0
  groups:
    count: 3-4 monochrome 4-connected path fragments, ordered left to right
    width: 2-3 columns each
    cells: 2-4 foreground cells per group; each forms a single connected component
    color: each group is monochrome (one non-bg color); colors may repeat across groups
    shape: path-like polyomino where every cell has degree 1 or 2; exactly 2 global endpoints
    endpoints: exactly 1 degree-1 cell on left edge column, exactly 1 on right edge column
    row_span: occupies 1-3 of the 3 rows; bounding box height 1-3
  separators: exactly 1 all-background column between each adjacent pair of groups [observed]
  layout: first group starts at column 0 [observed]
  output:
    height: same 3 rows
    width: sum of all group widths (separators removed)
    rule: keep first group at its original row offset; for each subsequent group left-to-right, shift it vertically so its left endpoint row aligns with the previous group's right endpoint row, then paste it immediately adjacent
    result: all groups form a single 4-connected component in the output
    vertical_shift_range: -2 to +2

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose N=3-4. For each group, pick a width (2-3) and a monochrome color (non-zero).
    2. Build the assembled output chain on a 3-row canvas by generating groups left to right.
       For each group, create a valid path polyomino (2-4 cells, 4-connected, all degree<=2, exactly 1 endpoint on each side column). Place it so the left endpoint row matches the previous group's right endpoint row, ensuring all cells stay in rows 0-2.
    3. Verify the assembled chain is a single connected component.
    4. Derive the input by extracting each group's columns, randomly shifting each group (except the first) back to its original unshifted row position, and inserting 1 blank separator column between groups.
    5. Validate that no group cells fall outside the 3-row grid after un-shifting (guaranteed by construction since groups originally fit before shifting).
